      "id": 3201,
      "part": "Bacterial Structure, Genetics & Lab",
      "tag": "Bacterial Structure, Genetics & Lab",
      "difficulty": "medium",
      "topic": "Bacterial growth curve",
      "stem": "A bacterial culture passes through lag, exponential (log), stationary, and death phases. During which phase are β-lactam antibiotics most effective, and during which phase do Bacillus and Clostridium begin to form spores?",
      "options": {
        "A": "Death phase; log phase",
        "B": "Lag phase; log phase",
        "C": "Log phase; stationary phase",
        "D": "Log phase; lag phase",
        "E": "Stationary phase; lag phase"
      },
      "answer": "C",
      "explanation": "In log phase, bacteria are actively dividing and building cell walls, so β-lactams work best. In stationary phase, nutrients are depleted and growth equals death. Spore formation (sporulation) starts here in Bacillus and Clostridium, and the large, slow-growing inocula of deep infections explain penicillin's reduced effect (Eagle effect).",
      "wrong": {
        "B": "Little wall synthesis happens in lag phase, and spores form later.",
        "E": "Beta-lactams work best when cells divide, which is log phase.",
        "A": "Dying cells are not actively building wall.",
        "D": "The first half is right, but sporulation begins in stationary phase."
      }
